WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.577 --> 00:00:03.577
(suspenseful music)

00:00:04.947 --> 00:00:06.910
- [Narrator] "Mindhunter"
is a dark, tense,

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:08.400
and thrilling crime show,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.860
but it contains almost no action.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.320
Besides the opening scene of season one,

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.360
we don't actually see
violence take place on screen,

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.360
instead most of the focus of
the show is on conversations

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.000
and information about
crime and psychology.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.210
So how does "Mindhunter" make information

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.890
and conversation so
dramatic and thrilling,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.540
drawing you into a story

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.390
that could easily be dry and academic?

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.800
In this video, I'll show
you six of the techniques

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.320
creators Joe Penhall and David Fincher use

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.930
in the writing, direction,
and cinematography

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.570
to draw you into the
world of "Mindhunter."

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.350
There will be spoilers for season one,

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.530
but while I'll use
examples from season two,

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.810
the video is free of major plot spoilers

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:50.923
for the second season.

00:00:53.093 --> 00:00:55.240
- I mean, we still can't
rule out a sequence killer,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.650
but if the perpetrator was a townie,

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.840
then the crime is random,
and there's no expectation

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.740
that it will repeat again.
- Here's my question.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.010
Shouldn't our funding
cover separate rooms?

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.300
- You don't like my company?

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.100
- [Narrator] Generally, what
grabs a viewer's attention

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.160
is conflict and drama, not
exposition or information.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.880
- You're really gonna go gothic

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.820
while I glean profound
insights from the Coed Killer?

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.100
- It's my day off.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.933
- [Holden] You're gonna be sorry.

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:19.080
- [Narrator] Most crime shows
rely on the drama created

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.650
in the pursuit of a criminal.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.200
While there are investigations
in "Mindhunter,"

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.360
much of the story lies outside
of those investigations,

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.560
focusing instead on the development

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.040
of psychological profiling
as a method of investigation.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.430
Understanding that story
requires a lot of exposition

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.300
and information to be
communicated to the audience.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.760
- Ooh, paperwork?
- Yes, it's hardly sexy,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.710
but I'm trying to put
together a list of questions

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.010
for the killers that you interview.

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.340
Things like a family history,
mood, thought patterns,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.877
before, during, and after the crime.

00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:51.540
- [Narrator] "Mindhunter"
makes that information

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.500
and exposition more interesting

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.760
by giving the information dramatic weight

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.640
in the character's lives.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:58.870
- You wanna know what I do?

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.410
You wanna see what I work on every night?

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:01.807
C'mon.

00:02:01.807 --> 00:02:03.810
- [Narrator] "Mindhunter"
spends a lot of time exploring

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:07.320
how the work the agents are
doing impacts them personally.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.690
Holden has to find the balance

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:11.060
between pursuing the
knowledge he wants to obtain

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.510
and the psychological toll
of trying to understand

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:16.180
how serial killers think will have on him.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:17.630
- There's nothing wrong--
- If what we're doing

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.780
doesn't get under your skin,

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.290
you're either more
screwed up than I thought

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.360
or you're kidding yourself.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.980
- [Narrator] Tench has to balance the good

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.180
he can do through his work

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.030
with the impact it has
on him and his family.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.530
- You have to bring that home?

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.610
What do you do with it?

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.283
- Examine it for clues.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.990
- You don't tell me a
thing about what you do.

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.230
- Now you know why.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.380
- You don't need to protect me, Bill.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:41.520
- I'm trying to protect everybody.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.080
- No, you need to protect your son.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.190
- [Narrator] And Dr. Carr
has to balance the work

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.140
against hiding who she is.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.640
- Do they know you're lesbian?

00:02:50.923 --> 00:02:52.620
- Of course not.
- Exactly.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.120
You can't be yourself.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.450
- [Narrator] For each member of the team,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.890
their journey into learning the psychology

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.450
of these criminals creates
conflict in their lives

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.330
that they must work to overcome.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.400
- Binary thinking could hamstring you

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.850
when it comes to critical
assessment of our subjects.

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.220
We need to understand the gray areas.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:10.893
- World is complex, I get that.

00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:14.750
- [Narrator] "Mindhunter"
uses its cinematography

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.810
to illustrate this drama
in the character's lives.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.660
Holden is the main
protagonist of season one

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.360
and so much of the season focuses

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.530
on the drama the work creates in his life,

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.490
and his fight to accomplish that work

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.230
within the bureaucracy of the FBI.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.610
In the traditional visual style,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.340
David Fincher, almost all
the shots are very steady,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.290
precise, dolly shots,

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.200
giving the camera a very
objective perspective.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.920
We see things clearly and from a distance

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.450
which is how Holden is trying

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.470
to approach the subject matter.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.660
Throughout the season,
the camera remains steady

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.520
even as Holden's emotional
state begins to deteriorate

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.480
until finally, he breaks and panics.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.640
In this moment at the end of season one,

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.130
we get the only two-handheld shots

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.927
in the entire first season of the show,

00:03:59.927 --> 00:04:03.177
(energetic rock music)

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.230
putting us directly inside Holden's panic.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.480
(energetic rock music)

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.390
Many shows frequently use
handheld cinematography,

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:18.250
but it often doesn't have
as much impact on the viewer

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:19.780
as these shots do here.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:21.860
By withholding a specific technique

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.580
throughout an entire season

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.400
and then strategically deploying it,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:27.930
it helps give the climax
of the first season

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.050
some serious dramatic weight.
(pulsing beat music)

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.370
In season two, Holden gets what he wants

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.120
from within the FBI.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.080
- These ideas aren't just progressive.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.980
They're practical and effective.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.690
You've proven that.
- I've tried,

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.430
but it's been a battle.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.070
- [Narrator] But the
audience now questions

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:46.260
whether or not that's a good
thing for his mental health.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.840
Camera shake can now be
used throughout season two

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.150
to occasionally signify
panic and remind us

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:54.490
that he could snap again.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.180
As Holden leaves his relationship behind

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:57.130
at the end of season one,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.370
Bill Tench and Wendy Carr's personal lives

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.070
become more of the focus in season two,

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.710
and the cinematography
is used to support this.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.240
Season two often uses slightly
more subjective perspectives

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:11.140
of these characters than
we see in season one,

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:12.660
placing the audience closer

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.797
to Bill and Wendy's emotional states.

00:05:15.797 --> 00:05:18.140
"Mindhunter" uses cinematography carefully

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.060
to draw us into the character's lives,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.530
showing us the toll that the
work has on their relationships

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.960
and the conflict it causes in them.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.350
Seeing this makes us more invested

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.290
both in their individual stories

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.053
and the work they're pursuing.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.130
As we've just seen, the
information and the work

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.480
creates drama in the character's
lives on a macro scale

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.490
across episodes and seasons,

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:43.820
but "Mindhunter" often increases the drama

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:45.890
of expositional conversations

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:48.810
by inserting smaller
conflicts and disagreements

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.860
between characters into individual scenes.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.060
In a scene like this of Holden and Tench

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.560
interviewing Richard Speck,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.750
the writers could've
relied on the simple drama

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.210
of what Richard Speck is talking about,

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.430
but they insert an
additional layer of conflict.

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.460
In this scene, it's Holden
and Tench's disagreement

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.451
over the interviewing style.

00:06:08.451 --> 00:06:10.184
- [Richard] Oh.

00:06:10.184 --> 00:06:11.620
- [Holden] Did you give it a name?

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.040
- [Narrator] Which is established simply

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.720
through a small look.

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.800
Inserting conflict in these
ways captures our attention,

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.050
pulling our focus towards the conflict

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:22.910
while the characters
talk about information

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.880
that moves the plot forward.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.430
This technique is used
for almost every interview

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.940
with a serial killer.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.230
- Showing mercy.
- What?

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.460
C'mon, kid.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.350
You don't expect us to buy your mercy

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:35.530
of horseshit.
- I think

00:06:35.530 --> 00:06:38.550
what Agent Tench means--
- But we can see examples

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.053
of this in many scenes
throughout the entire show.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:47.070
This scene from season one
inserts conflict into a scene

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.030
because Debby is trying to focus.

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.510
- What are you reading?
- And Holden wants to talk.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:54.020
This conflict creates a dramatic backdrop

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.600
for some exposition about psychology

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.430
that relates to a case
Holden is working on.

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.650
- I wonder if that's why everyone

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.223
is so confused about Roger.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.370
He's taking an activity normally reserved

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.370
for family members at home

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.930
and applying it to the wrong environment.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.420
- [Narrator] This scene from season two

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.630
does something similar,

00:07:13.630 --> 00:07:16.500
establishing interpersonal
conflict in the scene.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:18.130
- [Wendy] You know, whose test is this?

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.660
Yours or mine?

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:20.980
- Just trying to get to know you.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:23.600
- Feels a little like you're
trying to get to correct me.

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.460
- Well, would you rather
tell me about whatever it is

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.020
that you're burying your face in?

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.440
- [Narrator] That leads to exposition

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:32.420
about Dr. Carr's last interview.

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.280
- It's just a fine line of
giving them enough attention

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.650
so that they'll engage with you,

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:39.720
but also being able to
steer the conversation.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.850
And when it's over in
just a few minutes, it--

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.070
- Wasn't worth the effort?

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.120
- He was surprisingly
forthcoming in the beginning.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:46.390
He answered every question,

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.180
even volunteered information.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.240
- [Narrator] In nearly every scene,

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:52.140
there's some element of
interpersonal conflict

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:54.860
or disagreement between
two or more characters

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.690
that makes the conversations
more interesting and engaging.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.460
- The Geter boy's been
missing almost a week.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:02.530
We have to move, Holden.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.330
- You can't force a square
peg into a round hole

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:05.506
just because you prefer it

00:08:05.506 --> 00:08:07.030
to fit.
- Bill!

00:08:07.030 --> 00:08:09.310
- [Narrator] Sometimes, you
don't even need conflict,

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.010
just the possibility of
conflict to create tension.

00:08:13.010 --> 00:08:14.670
Tension is created in this scene

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:17.270
through the visual and contextual setup.

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.690
The subject doesn't want to be seen.

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:20.800
- Nerve damage from the shooting.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.050
Remember to face forward.

00:08:23.230 --> 00:08:25.750
- [Narrator] And so, Bill
Tench can't look at him.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.430
The gun in the truck raises the stakes.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:30.350
Nothing has to happen in the scene,

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:31.360
but there's a feeling

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.283
that things could escalate very easily.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.220
Without any action,
conversation after conversation

00:08:40.220 --> 00:08:41.587
could become monotonous.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:43.100
"Mindhunter" keeps things interesting

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.830
by setting conversations
in interesting places.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.810
Placing an argument between
Holden and his girlfriend

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.550
in a grocery store, or having
the agents walk and talk,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.380
instead of sitting in a meeting room

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.530
gives these conversations more variety,

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:56.780
and makes them more
interesting the audience.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:01.090
And sometimes, it just comes
down to using a location

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:02.540
in an interesting way.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.120
In this scene from season two,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:07.800
this conversation could've all
happened sitting at a desk,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.530
but instead, the location
of the conversation

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:14.200
moves four times over the
course of the entire scene,

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:15.740
allowing for a greater variety

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.950
of composition and character blocking.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.870
In this scene, the loud
environment of the bar

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:21.654
forces the characters to shout.

00:09:21.654 --> 00:09:23.050
(loud rock music)
- Maybe one of the things

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.573
wrong with our society
is all the criminality.

00:09:26.573 --> 00:09:29.447
- I'm talking from a
sociological perspective.

00:09:29.447 --> 00:09:31.220
- [Narrator] In this one, the environment

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:33.800
forces the characters to whisper.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:34.633
- We could do a short escrow.

00:09:34.633 --> 00:09:36.300
Be out in six weeks.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.353
- Nance, did something happen?

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.760
- I am thinking of Brian.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.110
- [Narrator] Both provide variety

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.480
and make the audience pay closer attention

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:47.763
to what's being said.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.980
Another way "Mindhunter"
keeps its conversations fresh

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:58.200
is through strong and
interesting visual composition,

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.910
often using character
blocking and composition

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:04.380
inspired by the noir crime
films of the '40s and '50s.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.540
These kinds of shots give the audience

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.370
more visual information
to examine and learn from.

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:12.380
Many shows or films can fall into the trap

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:14.380
of having conversation after conversation

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:16.460
take place with the
characters simply sitting

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:17.590
across from each other,

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:19.900
composing the scenes with basic, medium,

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:21.370
and over-the-shoulder shots,

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:23.810
but "Mindhunter" uses the
placement of characters

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.840
within wider shots to communicate subtext.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.990
For example, we can see the conflict

00:10:28.990 --> 00:10:31.400
between Holden and Tench in this shot,

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.060
simply through the distance
between them in the frame.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:36.720
This shot from season two

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.490
references the shot from season one

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:42.990
and communicates very effectively
how Dr. Carr is feeling

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.103
about her position in the team.

00:10:48.730 --> 00:10:52.070
Finally, "Mindhunter" rewards
those who are paying attention

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:55.020
to all the criminal psychology
that's being discussed

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:57.980
by giving its characters and
the audience opportunities

00:10:57.980 --> 00:10:59.610
to apply that knowledge

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:02.050
to understanding characters in scenes.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:04.490
- I watch all the cop shows on TV.

00:11:04.490 --> 00:11:05.323
- Do you know

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:08.600
Joseph Wambaugh?

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:09.433
(Ed chuckles)
"Police Story,"

00:11:09.433 --> 00:11:10.266
you ever watch that?

00:11:10.266 --> 00:11:11.560
- Huge fan!
- Oh.

00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:13.327
- Just came walking up off the street.

00:11:13.327 --> 00:11:15.220
"Hey, what happened, did anybody see?"

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:16.080
He'd been drinking.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.300
One of these guys who likes
talking to cops, you know?

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.010
- [Narrator] There are several small cases

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:23.640
throughout season one and an
essential case in season two.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:26.070
And the details of these cases often hinge

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:28.360
on what's being discussed
in the interviews

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:31.400
and discussions about criminal psychology,

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.610
but "Mindhunter" often
leaves those connections

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.420
between the interviews and
the cases up to the audience

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.690
to discover, sometimes episodes apart.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:42.690
Allowing the audience to draw
these connections on their own

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.460
increases their engagement.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:47.000
The short scenes at the
beginning of most episodes

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.570
that show a mysterious man
preparing for a killing

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:53.460
serve a similar purpose to the cases.

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:55.720
They're not just setting
up future seasons,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:59.400
but gives the audience an
opportunity to look for clues

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:02.350
and to try to psychologically
profile the character

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:03.790
on their own.

00:12:03.790 --> 00:12:05.710
There are plenty of small details

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:07.430
and connections you can catch

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.420
like the knots the killer
is practicing being similar

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.960
to the ones in this
photo from a crime scene

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.137
shown an episode later.

00:12:15.137 --> 00:12:17.860
"Mindhunter" is effectively
rewarding the audience member

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.690
who is paying attention
and examining the criminals

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:23.273
in the same way the lead characters are.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:27.390
Every story needs drama and conflict

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.200
to draw in its audience,

00:12:29.200 --> 00:12:30.810
but "Mindhunter's" subject matter

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:33.840
and non-traditional approach
to a crime show requires it

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:38.300
to be inventive with how it
creates that drama and conflict.

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:40.490
As we've seen here,
careful crafting of scenes

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.770
and intentional use of cinematography

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.120
are critical not only in establishing,

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:47.000
but selling that conflict,

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:50.330
pulling the audience deeper
into the minds of the characters

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.920
as they explore the minds

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.283
of some of the worst criminals on Earth.

